WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.941 --> 00:00:09.173 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the Europe capitals, episode one of six. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.828 --> 00:00:21.090 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We begin in the north: the North Atlantic capitals of Reykjavík, Dublin and
London, and the Nordic cluster of Helsinki, Oslo, Stockholm and Copenhagen.

3
00:00:21.757 --> 00:00:24.311 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Reykjavík into view.

4
00:00:28.504 --> 00:00:38.953 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Reykjavík stands on the south-west coast of Iceland, facing the Atlantic
across Faxaflói bay, the northernmost capital of a sovereign state.

5
00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:49.547 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name means "smoky bay", for the steam of its hot springs, and nearly all
its houses are heated with water from under the ground.

6
00:00:50.202 --> 00:00:58.944 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Reykjavík as the world's northernmost capital: a smoky bay of hot
springs under a roof of lava.

7
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.440 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Helsinki into view.

8
00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:13.620 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Helsinki sits on the coast of the Gulf of Finland in the south of Finland,
facing Estonia across the water.

9
00:01:14.284 --> 00:01:29.470 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From Helsinge, a parish name probably brought by settlers from Hälsingland in
Sweden, it was founded in 1550 to rival Tallinn and stayed a small town for
250 years.

10
00:01:30.129 --> 00:01:40.915 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Helsinki as the small town Russia made a capital in 1812, whose
neoclassical centre doubled for Moscow in Cold War films.

11
00:01:41.607 --> 00:01:43.987 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Oslo into view.

12
00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:57.377 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Oslo lies at the head of the Oslofjord in the south of Norway, with Sweden
just across the water to the east.

13
00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:09.628 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From 1624 to 1925 it was called Christiania, for the Danish king Christian IV.
The Nobel Peace Prize is awarded here.

14
00:02:10.291 --> 00:02:17.501 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Oslo as the city that was Christiania for three centuries, and where
the Nobel Peace Prize is handed out.

15
00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.209 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Stockholm into view.

16
00:02:24.559 --> 00:02:31.037 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Stockholm spreads across fourteen islands where Lake Mälaren meets the Baltic
Sea, in the east of Sweden.

17
00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:45.679 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
"Log islet" — stock, a log, plus holme, an islet: the city began on a
fortified island in 1252, and its archipelago holds about 30,000 more.

18
00:02:46.343 --> 00:02:58.673 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Stockholm as the city built on log islands, and the Vasa, a warship
raised again in 1961 that is still 98% original timber.

19
00:02:59.355 --> 00:03:01.189 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Copenhagen into view.

20
00:03:05.596 --> 00:03:12.713 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Copenhagen lies on the eastern coast of Zealand in Denmark, facing Sweden
across the Øresund.

21
00:03:13.362 --> 00:03:23.892 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Danish name København means merchants' harbour, and Tivoli Gardens opened
here in 1843.

22
00:03:24.548 --> 00:03:32.942 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Copenhagen as the merchants' harbour, home of Tivoli Gardens and the
Little Mermaid of 1913.

23
00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:36.653 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Dublin into view.

24
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:45.735 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dublin sits at the mouth of the River Liffey on the east coast of Ireland,
facing the Irish Sea.

25
00:03:46.399 --> 00:03:56.546 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From Irish Dubh Linn, "black pool", it began as a Viking settlement in 841,
and in Irish today it is mostly Baile Átha Cliath.

26
00:03:57.201 --> 00:04:04.318 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Dublin as the black pool that began as a Viking settlement, and the
home of the Book of Kells.

27
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.856 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring London into view.

28
00:04:11.052 --> 00:04:18.424 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
London stands on the Thames in the south-east of England, with its estuary
opening to the North Sea.

29
00:04:19.084 --> 00:04:26.166 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Londinium is pre-Roman and unexplained; the best guess is a Brittonic name
about the unfordable river.

30
00:04:26.827 --> 00:04:36.336 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember London as the largest city in the world for a hundred years, with
about 300 languages spoken in it and the world's first Underground beneath it.

31
00:04:41.373 --> 00:04:55.189 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Reykjavík is the far north-west, above the Atlantic. Dublin and London face
the Irish Sea, and Copenhagen, Stockholm, Oslo and Helsinki line the Baltic
side.

32
00:04:55.841 --> 00:05:09.018 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember smoky bay and hot springs, the Danes' merchant harbour, the log
islands of Stockholm, the Peace Prize of Oslo, and the tunnel under London
that was the world's first Underground.
